Use `useLayoutEffect` when you need to **measure or change the DOM before the browser paints**, so the user never sees an in-between frame. Typical cases:
- reading an element's size or position (`getBoundingClientRect`) to place a tooltip or popover;
- setting scroll position right after content changes (e.g. keeping a chat pinned to the bottom);
- anything where `useEffect` causes a visible flicker.

`useEffect` runs after paint, so it doesn't block rendering, which is what you want for data fetching, subscriptions, logging and timers. `useLayoutEffect` runs synchronously after the DOM updates and before paint, so slow work in it delays the frame. It also doesn't run on the server, which is why React warns about it in SSR. The rule: default to `useEffect`, and switch only when you see flicker or need a measurement before paint.

